{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"Premise VALIDATED by reading current main; a second layer of repetition found that the ticket title does not name.\n\nLayer 1 (as filed): four guards each call `_run_request_sites()` — `test_the_provenance_analysis_can_see_the_sites_it_judges` (tests/unit/test_subcall_class_selection.py:487), `test_no_run_request_inherits_the_raw_config_model` (495), `test_every_unfollowable_site_is_registered_with_a_reason\" (515), `test_neither_registry_has_stale_entries` (524). Each call re-walks `_SRC.rglob('*.py')` and re-parses every file mentioning `RunRequest(`.\n\nLayer 2 (not in the ticket): the cost of ONE call is itself dominated by repeated parsing. `_verdict`'s parameter-provenance branch (lines 448-450) re-walks `sorted(_SRC.rglob('*.py'))` and calls `ast.parse(caller_path.read_text())` on EVERY file in the tree, once per site whose config comes in as a parameter. `_functions()` (line 355) parses the whole tree again. That is why the ticket's own profile recorded ~8,033 parses for a single scan of a tree with far fewer files, and 32,124 for four scans.\n\nSo caching `_run_request_sites` per process (the filed fix) removes layer 1; memoising the per-path parse removes layer 2, which the four tests were each paying in full. The plan covers both, and the plan-review gate passed it with no blocking findings.\n\nSafety of caching: the analysis is a pure function of the committed `src/rebar` source. No test in the module mutates that tree, and the module's only autouse fixture (`class_table`, line 60) patches `llm_config._read_llm_file_table`, which the AST scan never consults."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1786605140133374001,"uuid":"38d8e29d-a9fb-4294-886a-de5a26de1524"}